\begin{abstract}
This document summarizes the data ingestion, cleaning, feature engineering, POI enrichment,
and modelling steps implemented in the project notebook `code\_improved.ipynb`. Key
artifacts (cleaned tables, feature tables, plots, and final predictions) are listed in the
Outputs section. Use this report to understand the methodology and reproduce results.
\end{abstract}

\section{Data Sources}
Input raw files are located under `data/` and initial ingests are stored in `bronze/`.
Primary tables used:
\begin{itemize}
	\item `transactions\_history\_final.csv` (transactions)
	\item `outlet\_master.csv` (outlet dimension)
	\item `outlet\_coordinates.csv` (lat/lon)
	\item `distributor\_seasonality\_details.csv` (seasonality per distributor)
	\item `holiday\_list.csv` (holidays)
\end{itemize}

\section{Pipeline Overview}
The notebook implements a three-layer pipeline:
\begin{description}
	\item[Bronze] Raw CSV ingestion: save pickle/CSV and a 1k sample for each raw file.
	\item[Silver] Data quality (DQ) checks and deterministic rejection rules; rejected rows
		are exported under `silver/rejected/` and clean tables saved in `silver/`.
	\item[Gold] Feature engineering and POI enrichment creating `gold/outlet\_features.csv`.
\end{description}

\section{Data Quality \& Cleaning}
The silver layer applies a standard set of DQ checks (nulls, format, referential integrity,
value ranges, outliers, duplicates). Rejected files are written to `silver/rejected/`.
Key saved artifacts (examples):
\begin{itemize}
	\item `silver/transactions\_clean.csv`
	\item `silver/outlet\_master.csv`
	\item `silver/outlet\_coordinates.csv`
	\item `silver/distributor\_seasonality.csv`
	\item `silver/holiday\_list.csv`
\end{itemize}

\section{Feature Engineering (Gold)}
Gold-stage steps include:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item Aggregate transactions to outlet-level features (avg/max/std monthly volume, total volume, active months, SKU breadth).
	\item Compute censorship indicators (censorship ratio, coefficient of variation) and a
				binary `is\_constrained` flag.
	\item Trend features (recent vs older averages $\rightarrow$ growth ratio).
	\item Attach outlet master attributes and coordinates; impute missing static fields where possible.
	\item Save final features to `gold/outlet\_features.csv` and `gold/outlet\_features.pkl`.
\end{enumerate}

\section{POI Enrichment}
Points-of-interest (POI) are downloaded via Overpass (cached). For outlets with coordinates,
the pipeline computes per-category POI counts within an outlet-dependent radius and nearest
POI distances using a BallTree (Haversine).

POI-derived columns are written into the gold table (columns prefixed with `poi\_` and
`nearest\_`). The POI cache path is `gold/poi\_raw.pkl`.

\section{Feature Selection \& Modelling}
Feature selection is informed by correlation analysis and a Random Forest importance study.
Modeling approach:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Train XGBoost regression on unconstrained outlets to predict `max\_monthly\_volume`.
	\item Fit two XGBoost models: mean objective (for central tendency) and a quantile-like
		objective (90th percentile) for an upper-bound estimate.
	\item Compute peer-group ceilings per `(Outlet\_Type, Outlet\_Size)` from unconstrained outlets.
	\item Ensemble predictions using a weighted combination of mean XGB, q90 XGB, and peer ceiling.
	\item Tune ensemble weights by 5-fold CV on the unconstrained set.
	\item Apply per-outlet peer-derived uplift and January adjustments (seasonality $\times$ growth $\times$ holiday factor),
		then enforce guardrails (floors and ceilings) to produce final `predicted\_jan\_2026\_potential`.
\end{itemize}

